# CORS is enforced by the browser, not by REST Client: these requests only show
# which headers the API answers with, which is what a browser would evaluate.

@baseUrl = http://localhost:3000

### 1. Preflight from the author site -> 204 with Access-Control-Allow-Origin, -Credentials, -Methods, -Headers
OPTIONS {{baseUrl}}/api/posts
Origin: http://localhost:5174
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type, x-requested-with

### 2. Preflight from the reader site -> same headers, echoing its own origin
OPTIONS {{baseUrl}}/api/posts/1/comments
Origin: http://localhost:5173
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type, x-requested-with

### 3. Preflight from an unknown origin -> no Access-Control-Allow-Origin: the browser would block
OPTIONS {{baseUrl}}/api/posts
Origin: http://evil.example
Access-Control-Request-Method: POST
Access-Control-Request-Headers: content-type, x-requested-with

### 4. Simple GET from an allowed origin -> Access-Control-Allow-Origin echoes it, plus Vary: Origin
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/posts
Origin: http://localhost:5173

### 5. Simple GET from an unknown origin -> 200 for REST Client, but without CORS headers the browser hides it
GET {{baseUrl}}/api/posts
Origin: http://evil.example
